          "annotations": [],
          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"occurrence_id\":\"bronte-tenant-of-wildfell-hall-80d98c28815cc254\",\"core_classification\":{\"label_support\":{\"truth_conditional\":4,\"performative\":1,\"exclamatory_reflexive\":0,\"other\":0},\"confidence\":0.96,\"analysis\":\"Gilbert’s mother directly avows her maternal love as an enduring emotional state and cites it as the basis for her concern about his future. This is overwhelmingly truth-conditional. The appeal weakly invokes her established maternal commitment, but the words themselves do not substantially undertake or renew an obligation. Nothing indicates reflex-like verbal discharge beyond emotionally invested assertion.\",\"ambiguity\":\"There is slight P pressure because the avowal supports a protective maternal stance, but no clear commitment is performed by the core phrase.\"},\"other_diagnosis\":{\"tpe_failure\":null,\"core_not_context\":null},\"utterance_status\":{\"status\":\"direct\",\"description\":\"A direct spoken avowal by Gilbert’s mother, syntactically embedded in the construction “You know … how I love you.”\"},\"contextual_interpretation\":\"The mother uses her love for Gilbert as argumentative authority for a serious remonstrance against his involvement with Eliza. Her affection appears sincere but is entangled with possessiveness, social judgment, and an attempt to influence his choices. Her later tears intensify the scene but do not make the love utterance independently expressive/reflexive.\",\"evidence\":[{\"evidence_id\":\"e1\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"“You know how deeply I have your advantage at heart, how I love you and prize you above everything else in the world”\",\"supports\":\"The utterance explicitly presents maternal love and valuation as true, strongly supporting T.\",\"confidence\":0.99},{\"evidence_id\":\"e2\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The avowal is coordinated with her desire to see Gilbert “well settled in life” and her grief at the prospect of his marrying Eliza or another local woman.\",\"supports\":\"The love claim functions as a premise for protective advice and attempted influence, creating weak P pressure but primarily contextualising the T avowal.\",\"confidence\":0.97},{\"evidence_id\":\"e3\",\"source\":\"local_text\",\"quotation_or_description\":\"The speech is an extended, reasoned remonstrance, followed later by tears; it is not described as involuntarily blurted or reflexively produced.\",\"supports\":\"Supports E=0: emotional intensity is present, but there is no independent evidence of expressive/reflexive core force.\",\"confidence\":0.95}],\"background_knowledge\":{\"used\":false,\"familiarity\":\"none\",\"confidence\":null,\"contribution\":null},\"ontology_assessment\":{\"fit\":\"natural\",\"diagnosis\":\"T naturally captures the avowal, while a low P score records the limited invocation of maternal commitment. Contextual manipulation and possessiveness do not require another core-force category.\",\"candidate_recurrent_dimension\":null},\"notes\":\"The later crying is treated as contextual evidence of emotion, not as sufficient evidence for E.\"}"
        }
      ],
